import java.util.*;

class Solution {

    public int[] bellmanFord(
        int n,
        int[][] edges,
        int source
    ) {

        // Distance array
        int[] dist = new int[n];

        Arrays.fill(dist, Integer.MAX_VALUE);

        // Source distance = 0
        dist[source] = 0;

        // Relax all edges V - 1 times.    A shortest path can have at most n - 1 edges if it doesn't contain a cycle.
        for (int i = 1; i <= n - 1; i++) {

            boolean updated = false;

            for (int[] edge : edges) {

                int u = edge[0];
                int v = edge[1];
                int weight = edge[2];

                // Skip if u is unreachable
                if (dist[u] == Integer.MAX_VALUE) {
                    continue;
                }

                // Relaxation
                if (dist[u] + weight < dist[v]) {

                    dist[v] = dist[u] + weight;

                    updated = true;
                }
            }

            // No changes → already shortest
            if (!updated) {
                break;
            }
        }

        // Check for negative cycle
        for (int[] edge : edges) {

            int u = edge[0];
            int v = edge[1];
            int weight = edge[2];

            if (dist[u] != Integer.MAX_VALUE &&
                dist[u] + weight < dist[v]) {

                // Negative cycle exists
                return new int[0];
            }
        }

        return dist;
    }
}

"Bellman-Ford is a single-source shortest path algorithm that works even when the graph contains negative edge weights. 
I initialize the source distance to zero and all other distances to infinity. 
Then I relax every edge V-1 times. 
Relaxation means checking whether going through the current edge gives a shorter distance.
 After these iterations, I perform one additional pass over all edges. 
 If any distance can still be reduced, the graph contains a negative-weight cycle. 
 Otherwise, the distance array contains the shortest paths."



